<p class="lead">Augusta Pines is a golf-course community, and for many homeowners the best part of the property is the back yard and the open view beyond it. That shapes how we light homes here. Spring Landscape Lighting focuses on outdoor living spaces, the frame around the view, and keeping light on your property so the course stays dark and the vista stays intact.</p>

<h2>Lighting that respects the view</h2>
<p>On a course-side lot, darkness beyond the yard is a feature, not a problem. Overlighting the back of the property creates a bright wall that blocks the view from inside and spills toward the fairway. A more effective approach is to light the near and middle ground softly so your eyes can still adjust to the open space beyond.</p>
<div class="grid-cards">
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Frame, don't flood</h3><p>Light trees and plantings at the edges of the yard to frame the view rather than floodlighting the lawn.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Shield and aim inward</h3><p>Use shielded fixtures aimed toward the house and plantings, not outward toward the course or neighbors.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Keep it warm and low</h3><p>Warm 2700K light at modest output reads as calm from inside and from across the course.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Back-yard outdoor living</h2>
<p>Patios, covered porches, outdoor kitchens and pools are where Augusta Pines homeowners tend to spend their evenings. These spaces need comfortable, layered light: task lighting for the grill, soft downlight for dining, hardscape lights tucked under seat walls and step edges, and accents on nearby trees. The goal is enough light to cook and talk without glare in anyone's eyes. Our <a href="/services/patio-pool-lighting/">patio and pool lighting</a> page explains how we approach it.</p>
<p>Zoning and dimming are especially useful here. A dinner setting, a later evening setting and a "lights off, enjoy the view" setting are easy to create with <a href="/services/smart-lighting-controls/">smart lighting controls</a>.</p>

<h2>Light trespass toward the course</h2>
<p>Light trespass is light that crosses your property line where it is not wanted. On golf-course lots it is easy to create, because there is nothing behind the yard to stop it. Common culprits include wide-beam uplights in open lawn, floodlights mounted on the back of the house, and tree fixtures aimed through thin canopies into open sky. We control it with beam selection, glare shields, careful aiming and a final adjustment after dark viewed from the yard's edge as well as from the patio.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Tip:</strong> Preview how different color temperatures feel before choosing. Our <a href="/tools/color-temperature-guide/">color temperature explorer</a> compares 2200K through 5000K on stone, plants and water.</div>

<p>Don't overlook the front. A balanced design includes <a href="/services/architectural-lighting/">architectural lighting</a> at the entry so the home feels as finished from the street as it does from the course. Spring Landscape Lighting can plan both together.</p>

